# focus — a modus mentis that turns away from the clicked object observes another instead
#
# Ripelore is asked to focus on the test door and turns away (--refuse-focus stands in for the
# choice judge answering "turn elsewhere", which --playground never does). It is not a dead end:
# Ripelore picks another unobserved object, and one text both dismisses the door and observes the
# new one — so the block carries a keyword, and the keyword names the new object, never the door.
# The follow-up observations run after it as they do after a kept focus.
#
# Which object it turns to is the playground's seeded draw, so it moves whenever an earlier draw is
# added or removed — the arrival's added person observation is why it is the butterfly now.
#
# The Test Yard, not the Test Room: the area introduction crosses off every object it names, and in
# the four-object Test Room that left nothing unobserved to turn to — the keyword-less "not
# interested" branch, not this one. The door is clicked by name for the same reason: it is one of
# several keywords the introduction links.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --location-type test --location-id 0 --start-area "Test Yard" --period dawn --refuse-focus

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60
advance 12 180

# Observe the door with Ripelore; it turns to the butterfly instead.
click keyword door
choose 1
choose 0
advance 12 180
expect I am not interested in a test door. My attention shifts to a butterfly

# The turn carried the new object's keyword, wired to the butterfly: observing through "butterfly"
# focuses on it (and, under --refuse-focus, turns away from it in turn).
click keyword butterfly
choose 1
choose 1
advance 12 180
expect I am not interested in a butterfly

quit
